<h2 id="parallel">12.2 Parallel by default</h2>
<p>
  The second half of the discipline: independent requests must not wait for
  <em>each other</em>. Sequential awaits in one component are the classic server-side
  waterfall - each fetch starts only after the previous one finishes, and the region's
  wait is the <em>sum</em> of its requests instead of the <em>max</em>:
  <Chip k="react" />
  <Chip k="critical" />
</p>

<Compare>
<CompareCol kind="bad" label="✕ Serialized: wait is the sum">
    <Snippet>
    <pre
                class="code" is:raw
              ><span class="filename">page.tsx</span><span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">Page</span>({ params }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { id } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> params;

  <span class="tok-com">/* Each await gates the next. Three
     independent questions, asked one
     at a time: 300 + 400 + 500 ms. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> program = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getProgram</span>(id);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> stats = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getStats</span>(id);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> rows = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getRows</span>(id);
  <span class="tok-com">// …render everything from here</span>
}</pre>
    </Snippet>
  
</CompareCol>
<CompareCol kind="good" label="✓ Parallel: wait is the max">
    <Snippet>
    <pre
                class="code" is:raw
              ><span class="filename">page.tsx: siblings parallelize naturally</span><span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">Page</span>({ params }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { id } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> params;

  <span class="tok-com">/* Three self-fetching server components
     (§13). The server renders siblings
     concurrently: all fetches start at t₀. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramHeader</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={id} /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramStats</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={id} /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ReportRows</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={id} /&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    </Snippet>
    <Snippet>
    <pre
                class="code" is:raw
              ><span class="filename">when one component needs several answers</span><span class="tok-kw">const</span> [program, stats] = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">Promise.all</span>([
  <span class="tok-fn">getProgram</span>(id),
  <span class="tok-fn">getStats</span>(id),
]);</pre>
<CodeCaption>
  Inside a single component: start all the requests first, then await them together.
</CodeCaption>
    </Snippet>
  
</CompareCol>
</Compare>

<p>
  Two mechanics worth knowing precisely.
  <strong>Sibling async server components render concurrently</strong> - composing
  self-fetching components (<a href="13-minimize-the-wires.html">§13</a>) gives you
  parallelism for free, which is another reason independence beats drilling.
  <Chip k="react" />
  <Chip k="critical" /> And
  <strong>a call into the deduped data layer starts the request</strong> whether or not
  you await it - so when a fetch is genuinely slow and needed below, you can kick it off
  early (<code>const p = getRows(id)</code> with no await) and let the component that
  needs it await the same deduped promise later. Start early, await late, and only where
  needed.
</p>
<p>
  Genuinely <em>dependent</em> requests - B needs A's answer - are the one honest
  waterfall. The discipline there:
  <strong>confine the chain to the smallest region that needs its result.</strong>
  <Chip k="react" />
  <Chip k="very" />
</p>

<Snippet>
<pre
            class="code" is:raw
          ><span class="filename">ProgramManagerCard.tsx - the chain waits here, not at the page</span><span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramManagerCard</span>({ programId }) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* getUser genuinely depends on the program's answer. Because the chain
     lives in this leaf, only this card pays for two round trips - the page,
     the stats, and the table never knew. Both calls are deduped (§14). */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> program = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getProgram</span>(programId);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> manager = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getUser</span>(program.managerId);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ContactCard</span> <span class="tok-prop">person</span>={manager} /&gt;;
}</pre>
</Snippet>

<h2 id="parallel-routes">12.3 Parallel routes: independence with a file address</h2>
<p>
  <a href="09-containers-content-shifting.html#layouts">§9.4</a> put the containers in the
  layout; <a href="13-minimize-the-wires.html">§13</a> made the regions self-fetching;
  <a href="10-suspense-streaming.html">§10</a> gave each one a boundary.
  <strong>Parallel routes</strong> are the framework formalizing that whole pattern as
  filesystem structure: a layout declares named <em>slots</em> (<code>@header</code>,
  <code>@summary</code>, <code>@table</code>), each slot is its own mini-page, and the
  framework fills them
  <strong
    >independently - each with its own <code>loading.tsx</code>, its own
    <code>error.tsx</code>, its own streaming lifecycle.</strong
  >
  <Chip k="next" />
  <Chip k="very" />
</p>

