    &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"flex h-full flex-col"</span>&gt;
      {header}
      &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"grid grid-cols-[1fr_320px] gap-6 min-h-0 grow"</span>&gt;
        &lt;main <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"overflow-y-auto"</span>&gt;{children}&lt;/main&gt;
        &lt;aside&gt;{summary}&lt;/aside&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
</Snippet>

<p>
  When to reach for a slot instead of a plain component: when the region deserves
  <strong>route-level independence</strong> - its own <code>loading.tsx</code>/<code
    >error.tsx</code
  >
  files, participation in navigation, or different content per sub-route. Nick's canonical
  example: a page header. If the header is <em>static</em>, it's just layout markup - no
  slot needed. If the header <em>depends on server data</em> (program name, status badge,
  owner), a <code>@header</code> slot gives it an error and loading lifecycle that can't
  take the table down with it, and vice versa. One convention matters in practice:
  <strong><code>default.tsx</code></strong> is what a slot renders when the current URL
  doesn't match anything inside it (typically: re-render nothing-specific, or return
  <code>null</code>) - omit it and navigation into unmatched states 404s the whole route.
  Define it for every slot. <Chip k="next" />
  <Chip k="critical" />
</p>

