<h2 id="client">12.4 The client has waterfalls too</h2>
<p>
  Everything above has a client-side mirror, and it's the expensive one - each client-side
  gate is a full network round trip <em>plus</em> a render cycle. The patterns to hunt:
  <strong>dependent query chains</strong> (<code>useQuery</code>/<code>useSWR</code> hooks
  gated on each other's results - profile → organization id → dashboard data, each hop
  serialized after hydration), and <strong>fetch-after-mount cascades</strong>, where a
  component fetches, renders children, and the children then begin <em>their</em> fetches.
  Our audit found 53 <code>enabled:</code>-gated dependent queries in one app - 53
  serialized round trips that a server component tree would have resolved in one pass.
  <Chip k="react" />
  <Chip k="critical" />
</p>
<p>
  The fixes, in order: <strong>move the chain to the server</strong>, where the dependency
  is an in-datacenter hop inside one request (<a href="#parallel">§12.2</a>'s
  dependent-chain pattern) - this is the structural fix and it's usually available;
  <strong>collapse the chain's key</strong>, so the client asks one question ("dashboard
  for current user") and the server resolves the intermediate steps; and where client
  fetching is legitimate (<a href="14-dedupe-caching.html#client">§14.3</a>),
  <strong>make sibling queries independent</strong> - two hooks with no
  <code>enabled:</code> coupling fire in parallel.
</p>

