The same three reads, four ways. Chained on the client, each one waits for the last, so
    latency stacks. Moved to the server, the dependency chain becomes in-datacenter hops
    inside a single round trip. Independent reads on the server go one better: each
    region's component fetches in parallel and streams into its own Suspense boundary the
    moment it resolves (<a href="10-suspense-streaming.html">§10</a>). And where client
    fetching is legitimate, decoupling the queries lets them start together, so the user
    waits for the slowest one, not the sum.
</Fragment>
